WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.720
Socotra is a unique island. An island that&nbsp;
remained isolated from the world until 2002, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.160
a small airport was built for the first time. With&nbsp;
its landscape, Socotra has stimulated over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:17.360
centuries the imagination of many travelers with&nbsp;
its peculiar umbrella-shaped trees: the Socotra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:22.800
Dragon Trees. According to one of the old local&nbsp;
stories, the first seed of Dragon Tree, sprouted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.400
in the exact spot where two brothers - Darsa and&nbsp;
Samha - chose to fight to death. A story that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.520
kinda reminds us of Cain and Abel in the Bible.&nbsp;
In Arabic, this peculiar kind of tree is known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.880
the blood of the two brothers. For centuries the&nbsp;
islanders have used the reddish sap of this tree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.240
as an ointment with magical properties, and it&nbsp;
is no coincidence that the dragon tree has become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:50.640
over time the most recognizable symbol of Socotra&nbsp;
Island, an island endowed with a biodiversity more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.480
alien than terrestrial. Socotra is an island&nbsp;
blessed with more than 700 endemic species of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:03.360
flora and fauna, unique in their kind, and thanks&nbsp;
to which in 2008 UNESCO appointed the island a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.200
World Heritage Site to be preserved and protected.&nbsp;
Nature is the true master of Socotra, a beauty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.720
that has always inspired me to get interested&nbsp;
in the events of this island, so much so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.760
I hope one day to be able to visit it, although&nbsp;
I don't see that as feasible thing at the moment&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:25.280
bizarre plants, untouched and tremendously&nbsp;
fascinating shores, and unspoiled highlands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.640
For many, Socotra is a piece of paradise, but for&nbsp;
others it's just a barren land to be exploited.&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.800
Located in the Indian Ocean, in a position&nbsp;
which makes it geographically part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.560
African continent, Socotra is closer to Somalia&nbsp;
than to Yemen: from the Somali coast, the island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.200
is about two hundred and forty kilomiters away,&nbsp;
while from the Yemeni coast it is more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.800
double. This distance has contributed in large&nbsp;
part to the isolation and sense of mystery that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:57.040
Arab, Persian and African sailors felt about&nbsp;
such a remote island. The archipelago of Socotra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:02.960
is formed by three other small strips&nbsp;
of land, Abd Al Kuri, Darsa and Samha.&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.160
The 60,000 inhabitants living on the island&nbsp;
have always relied on fishing and on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:15.040
cultivation of date palms, and despite belonging&nbsp;
politically to Yemen since 1990, the islanders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.720
half Shiite and half Sunni, boast their own&nbsp;
culture and a different language, Socotri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.480
a Semitic language even older than Arabic and&nbsp;
whose origin is still an arcane mystery. this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:33.360
day Socotra finds itself embroiled in a civil war&nbsp;
that has been ravaging Yemen since 2015. Socotra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.400
became the epicenter of a power struggle between&nbsp;
the Houthi rebels and the Central Government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.080
but it became also the place of another secondary&nbsp;
conflict: the one between the United Arab Emirates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:49.280
and the Yemeni government of Mansur Hadi. The&nbsp;
situation in Yemen has been tragic for over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:55.840
six years, to be exact since Hadi was forced by&nbsp;
Shiite rebels belonging to the Ansar Allah group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.760
better known as Houthi, to resign from its&nbsp;
presidency, abandon the capital San'a and leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.480
it under the control of the rebels. The war in&nbsp;
Yemen is better known as a proxy war, a conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.280
where the real actors are quite different from&nbsp;
those facing each other on the battlefield: under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:17.200
the surface Yemen is actually disputed on one side&nbsp;
by the Saudis, supported by the rest of the gulf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:23.040
countries and the US, which approved the official&nbsp;
government of Hadi. On the other side there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.880
Iran, which in historical contrast to the Saudi&nbsp;
monarchy, supports the rebellion of the Houthis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:34.880
seeing it as a possibility to expand its sphere of&nbsp;
influence directly in the Arabian peninsula. We'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:42.480
talk more about the war in Yemen in another video.&nbsp;
Known as "the jewel of the Gulf of Aden," Socotra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.920
is situated off the Horn of Africa, overlooking&nbsp;
the Bab el-Mandeb Strait, which connects the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:53.360
Red Sea to both the Gulf of Aden and the Arabian&nbsp;
Sea. The attentions surrounding Socotra are many:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.880
Every day, more than three million oil barrels&nbsp;
travel from the Persian Gulf to the Indian Ocean.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:04.560
Ships on this route travel west across the Arabian&nbsp;
Sea into the Gulf of Aden, then turn north,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.280
entering the Red Sea through the narrow Bab&nbsp;
el-Mandeb passage before going up to the Suez&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.800
Canal. The Socotra Archipelago occupies a critical&nbsp;
position on the route to Bab el-Mandeb. Socotra's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:20.000
central location also makes it an ideal launching&nbsp;
point for interdicting pirates and smugglers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.800
It protects the trade routes and all the fishing&nbsp;
communities along the Horn of Africa and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.000
southern coast of Yemen. From Iran to Somalia,&nbsp;
and from Eritrea to Qatar and India, for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.200
international and regional powers have struggled&nbsp;
to establish bases around the Horn of Africa. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:41.920
have a strategic advantage over all these nations&nbsp;
is the United Arab Emirates, which since 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:47.680
has maintained a military presence on the island&nbsp;
to support the STC, the Southern Transitional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.800
Council, an entity whose purpose is to achieve&nbsp;
the separation of South Yemen from the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:58.080
nation. The Emirates' presence has only increased&nbsp;
divisions on the island: Socotra's governor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.920
Ramzi Mahrous, has always rejected the presence&nbsp;
of the STC, being in his eyes a clear threat to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:09.520
his figure; on the other hand, however, many&nbsp;
islander are polarized on the choice to adopt:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.240
to support the Transitional Council of the&nbsp;
South, and therefore the Arab Emirates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:20.160
or to refuse the interference of a foreign country&nbsp;
in the island's affairs. Separated from food and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:25.520
fuel supplies since the outbreak of the War, the&nbsp;
Island was left at the mercy of its own fate:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.840
The absence of Houthis on the island couldn't&nbsp;
grant a casus belli to intervene militarily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:35.520
and so the Emirates - with the initial support&nbsp;
of the Saudis - used the excuse of humanitarian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:40.560
aid to set foot on the island and secure&nbsp;
the support of part of the population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:47.600
They found the opportunity in 2015, when in the&nbsp;
space of a few days Socotra ended up being hit by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:52.960
two tropical cyclones that brought destruction&nbsp;
and further misery to the population that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:58.480
already been living on less than a dollar a day&nbsp;
for decades. Masquerading it as a large-scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:04.160
humanitarian intervention by the Red Crescent,&nbsp;
Abu Dhabi deployed over 5,000 troops to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:09.440
island without the coordination or approval of&nbsp;
either the Yemeni government or the governor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.440
of Socotra. In 2018, the United Arab Emirates&nbsp;
under the guise of training local Yemeni forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.960
in Socotra reportedly began to weave a series of&nbsp;
personal interests on the island: First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:25.840
they did it to strengthen military connections&nbsp;
with all their bases along the Horn of Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:32.000
such as the Berbera base in Somaliland, the Assab&nbsp;
naval base in Eritrea, and other military barracks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.360
in Puntland, in Somalia. From a strategic point of&nbsp;
view, Socotra provides a vital link between these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:42.160
regions and would allow the Emirate navy&nbsp;
to conduct important security operations:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:47.440
in terms of contrasting piracy, the island has&nbsp;
long been a docking base for many of the Somali&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:52.720
pirates who operate far and wide along the&nbsp;
Horn of Africa as far as the Omani coast. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:58.960
stable presence would also allow the emirates, and&nbsp;
implicitly the Saudis, to keep out Iranian boats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:04.160
that transport arms and supplies to the Houthi&nbsp;
rebels. In a proxy war against Iran, all of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:09.360
would strengthen Saudi Arabia. In this conflict,&nbsp;
Socotra would serve as an excellent support base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:14.400
from which to launch rockets against the Houthis&nbsp;
in Yemen. The fact that the Emirates use Socotra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.560
as a military base is still a matter of debate,&nbsp;
given the logistical problems of the island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:23.360
such as those dictated by the climate and&nbsp;
monsoons which, for six months of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.240
make operations in the area very difficult. The&nbsp;
interest of the United Arab Emirates in Socotra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:33.200
has been interpreted as a challenge by Saudi&nbsp;
Arabia: by occupying the island, the Emirates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:38.640
are trying to block the expansion and the regional&nbsp;
domination of Saudi Arabia in the entire region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:44.480
consolidating their presence in the triangle&nbsp;
that at its vertices touches Aden, Socotra and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.920
the small island of Barim. And so, the reasons&nbsp;
that led Abu Dhabi to be guaranteed a 99-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:56.080
concession by President Hadi would be mainly&nbsp;
economic: Controlling Socotra would mean acquiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:01.520
a large slice of the monopoly in the traffic of&nbsp;
oil and gas that passes through Bab El Mandeb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:07.360
a move made in total autonomy and in contrast with&nbsp;
both Iran and Saudi Arabia. With the occupation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:13.360
of Socotra, the Emirates would also be able to&nbsp;
acquire kilometers and kilometers of Indian Ocean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:18.240
through the strengthening of an Exclusive Economic&nbsp;
Zone in the territorial waters around the island.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:23.520
For the Saudis, the Emirates are an alliance of&nbsp;
convenience. It's not by chance that the Emirates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:29.440
are planning to annex Socotra by organizing a&nbsp;
referendum in order to make the islanders decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:35.920
whether to become officially part of the Emirates&nbsp;
or remain at the mercy of the uncertainty of war;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:41.120
Feeling neglected by Hadi's central government,&nbsp;
the Socotrians had no choice but to welcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:46.720
Abu Dhabi's investments. The Emiratis have not&nbsp;
only built basic infrastructure, such as roads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:52.080
hospitals and schools, but they have also paid the&nbsp;
salaries of doctors, policemen and bureaucrats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:58.240
creating new opportunities, for a total of 5,000&nbsp;
new jobs. After 2 weeks in May 2018, the Emirati&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:03.520
troops abandoned the island, they got replaced by&nbsp;
Saudi troops but they still left behind officials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:08.800
who are working for the Khalifa Foundation of&nbsp;
Abu Dhabi, a government-sponsored charity. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:13.680
Governor Mahrus, however, such charitable&nbsp;
organizations would conceal a more devious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:20.480
objective: that is, to buy the loyalty of Socotra&nbsp;
residents, and allow UAE traders and investors to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:26.080
conduct business on the island under their cover.&nbsp;
These organizations would facilitate the purchase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:31.920
of land to wealthy Emiratis, despite the presence&nbsp;
of a government decree prohibiting the sale of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:37.200
any land on the island. Many of these lots would&nbsp;
belong to protected coastal areas, in which the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:43.040
construction of buildings, and changes to the&nbsp;
landscape are prohibited; yet, when buying land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:48.800
with prices ranging from hundreds of thousands to&nbsp;
tens of millions of dollars, Emiratis have never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:55.200
hidden their goals of building private houses and&nbsp;
tourist resorts. Emirates, and no longer Yemen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:00.640
seem to have become the headquarter of the island:&nbsp;
witness to this are the Emirates companies which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:07.280
have weekly flights between Hadiboh, capital of&nbsp;
Socotra, and Abu Dhabi. The Emiratis are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:13.280
aware that Socotra may become a great catalyst for&nbsp;
tourism. Before the civil war, the island operated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:19.360
a particular kind of ecotourism, respectful of the&nbsp;
environment, which granted a small number of visas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:24.640
per year, especially to scientists to facilitate&nbsp;
their study of Socotra's ecosystem. All of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:30.320
may disappear with the Emirati occupation and&nbsp;
with their plans to favor luxury resorts all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:35.040
over the island, just like they already did&nbsp;
in Dubai with their artificial archipelagos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:41.360
I might say that the objective is very simple:&nbsp;
to transform Socotra into a Dubai 2.0. To do so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:46.160
Socotra's deputy governor for environment&nbsp;
and development, Abduljameel Mohammed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:51.520
as well as Emirati companies have reportedly&nbsp;
already begun excavating several sections of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:57.200
the island's national park in preparation for&nbsp;
a series of five-star resorts. Leaving aside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:03.120
the contradictions of seeing five-star hotels in&nbsp;
villages still destroyed by monsoons and poverty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:09.840
all this has raised a lot of criticism in the&nbsp;
island: as reported by local sources, the Emirates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:15.440
didn't obtain any permission from Yemen to start&nbsp;
their construction projects. In addition to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:21.760
some reports highlighted that several endangered&nbsp;
species - both plants and animals - have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:27.360
taken from the island and resettled in the&nbsp;
Emirates. Many Socotrians saw this move as not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:32.880
only a violation of the island's identity but&nbsp;
also as an offense against their environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:37.920
something made possible by the position of&nbsp;
supremacy enjoyed by Emirati officials there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:42.400
The possibility of the island being used&nbsp;
as a new destination for vacationing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:47.840
has raised concerns related to pollution&nbsp;
of a fragile ecosystem. And maybe this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:53.120
the saddest element of the story. Also, the&nbsp;
fact that the Emirates, several months later,

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:54.800
sent troops on the Island, again

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:56.400
doesn't bode well.

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:01.920
Abu Dhabi does not seem willing to leave the bone&nbsp;
- which is metaphorically the island - in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:07.520
hands of the Saudis. Because this is what it’s all&nbsp;
about: a bone, an island that is at risk of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:12.720
stripped of all its beauty for mere geopolitical&nbsp;
and economic plots. Yemeni journalist Mohammed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:20.000
al-Rumim earlier this year said, "The UAE is not&nbsp;
a friend of Socotra. They're only interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:26.000
colonizing it." The Emirates have always denied&nbsp;
the accusations of neo-colonialism. But what seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:31.200
more and more certain is that the Emirates&nbsp;
intend to keep their presence on the island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:36.560
with or without Yemeni approval. On the one hand&nbsp;
this allows the Emirates to avoid open conflicts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:41.840
with the Arabs and other competitors, such as&nbsp;
Israel which seems to have an intelligence base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:48.560
on the island, on the other hand it relegates the&nbsp;
islanders to pawns in a geopolitical chess game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:52.320
whose outcome could prove catastrophic&nbsp;
for their traditional lifestyle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:58.800
and for the island they call home. Only time will&nbsp;
tell which side will prevail. They say that time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:03.840
is a gentleman. I have serious doubts that it&nbsp;
will be so in this case. Thanks to everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:08.000
for listening and keeping me company until&nbsp;
the end. See you in the next video. Bye.

